# clang-tidy settings for the host-compilable product code
# (bootloader: sha256 image rollback update ob_check; secure_link: aes128 aes_cmac secure_link fcnt_store).
# Checks: CERT-style rules, bugprone checks and the clang static analyser.
# Four checks are switched off on purpose. Each one fired on this code, every finding was
# read, and none was a defect. The reasons:
#
# bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters (5 findings): a design warning about neighbouring
#   same-type parameters. Looked at one by one: swapping dev_id/fcnt in ctr_xor passed every
#   test, so a known-answer test was added (it now fails on that swap); swapping digest and
#   pubkey in image_check_signature is rejected by gcc (-Werror=stringop-overread); swapping
#   two arguments of sl_open in a test call is caught by the tests; ob_check and ob_is_fatal
#   are called only from bootloader/main.c, which is not built on the PC (call order read by hand).
# bugprone-implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result (4): int multiplications such as 4*i with
#   i from 0 to 15, used as array offsets in sha256.c and aes128.c. The largest value is 63. It
#   fires on a 64-bit PC; on the 32-bit target int and ptrdiff_t have the same width.
# bugprone-branch-clone (1): aes_cmac.c, the CMAC padding rule. Two branches have the same body
#   (full block, and index below the length). Read and correct; the RFC 4493 tests pass.
# clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.DeprecatedOrUnsafeBufferHandling (2): memset and memcpy in
#   image.c. It asks for C11 Annex K functions, which the ARM and Linux toolchains do not have.
#   The memset clears a public hash; both sizes are constants.
Checks: '-*,cert-*,bugprone-*,clang-analyzer-*,-bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,-bugprone-implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result,-bugprone-branch-clone,-clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.DeprecatedOrUnsafeBufferHandling'
WarningsAsErrors: '*'
